---
<Layout title={`${pageTitle} | Portaloo Online`} description={pageDescription}><script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(structuredData)} /><main class="guide-page"><section class="guide-hero"><div class="container hero-container"><nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><span>/</span><span>Portable Toilets for Festivals and Large Events</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">Festival planning guide</p><h1>Portable Toilets for Festivals and Large Events</h1><p class="hero-intro">Plan portable toilets, urinals, accessible facilities, campsites, handwashing, servicing and site layouts for festivals and large outdoor events.</p><div class="guide-meta"><span>10 minute read</span><span>Updated October 2026</span></div></div></section><div class="container article-layout"><aside class="contents"><div class="contents-card"><p class="contents-title">In this guide</p><nav aria-label="Guide contents"><a href="#quick-answer">Quick answer</a><a href="#numbers">Toilet numbers</a><a href="#mix">Choosing the mix</a><a href="#accessible">Accessible facilities</a><a href="#campsites">Festival campsites</a><a href="#layout">Site layout</a><a href="#servicing">Servicing and cleaning</a><a href="#handwashing">Handwashing</a><a href="#contingency">Contingency planning</a><a href="#checklist">Planning checklist</a><a href="#faqs">FAQs</a><a href="#find-suppliers">Find suppliers</a></nav></div></aside><article class="guide-content"><section class="summary-box" id="quick-answer"><h2>Quick answer</h2><p>Festival toilet planning must consider attendance, opening hours, food and alcohol, campsites, accessible provision, workers, cleaning, emptying and the distances across the site.</p><ul><li>Calculate from the maximum expected attendance</li><li>Increase provision for longer events serving food and alcohol</li><li>Include urinals and accessible toilets</li><li>Plan cleaning and waste removal throughout the event</li><li>Distribute facilities around large sites</li><li>Keep servicing routes separate from public crowd flows where possible</li></ul></section>
<section id="numbers"><h2>How many toilets does a festival need?</h2><p>The Purple Guide public sanitation summary gives a typical local-event starting point of one female toilet per 100 attendees, one male toilet per 500 attendees and one urinal per 150 attendees, plus at least one dedicated accessible toilet.</p><p>Local-authority guidance based on the Purple Guide increases provision for longer events, especially where food and alcohol are served.</p><div class="table-wrapper"><table><thead><tr><th>Event conditions</th><th>Female toilets</th><th>Male toilets</th><th>Urinals</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Less than 6 hours</td><td>1 per 100</td><td>1 per 500</td><td>1 per 150</td></tr><tr><td>6+ hours, little or no food/alcohol</td><td>1 per 85</td><td>1 per 425</td><td>1 per 125</td></tr><tr><td>6+ hours with food and alcohol</td><td>1 per 75</td><td>1 per 400</td><td>1 per 100</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Use the maximum number expected on site at one time and confirm the final calculation with the supplier and relevant event authority.</p></section>
<section id="mix"><h2>Choosing the right mix of facilities</h2><div class="card-grid"><article><h3>Standard portable toilets</h3><p>The main cubicle provision for most festivals, with quantities based on attendance and duration.</p></article><article><h3>Urinals</h3><p>Useful for reducing demand on cubicles, but they do not replace appropriate toilet provision.</p></article><article><h3>Accessible toilets</h3><p>Dedicated accessible units should be provided and clearly signposted.</p></article><article><h3>Toilet trailers and blocks</h3><p>Useful for VIP, hospitality, staff or areas with suitable utility and access arrangements.</p></article></div></section>
<section id="accessible"><h2>Accessible festival toilets</h2><p>Provide accessible toilets on firm, level ground with a clear step-free route. Large or dispersed festivals may need accessible units in several zones, including the main arena and accessible campsite.</p><p>Show the locations in access information and site maps, provide lighting, and keep routes clear throughout the event.</p></section>
<section id="campsites"><h2>Toilets for festival campsites</h2><p>Campsites operate for longer periods than the arena and create overnight and morning demand peaks. Dover District Council publishes campsite guidance of one female toilet per 75 attendees, one male toilet per 150, plus one urinal per 250.</p><ul><li>Plan for overnight access and lighting</li><li>Place handwashing close to toilet areas</li><li>Provide accessible facilities within or close to accessible camping</li><li>Schedule early-morning cleaning and emptying</li><li>Consider showers and additional welfare for multi-day camping</li></ul></section>
<section id="layout"><h2>Festival toilet layout and positioning</h2><ul><li>Distribute facilities across entrances, arenas, food areas and campsites</li><li>Avoid one distant central compound</li><li>Use firm ground and plan for wet weather</li><li>Provide lighting, visible signs and clear queue space</li><li>Keep toilets away from food preparation areas</li><li>Maintain tanker and service-vehicle access</li><li>Avoid routes that conflict with emergency access or dense crowd flows</li></ul></section>
<section id="servicing"><h2>Servicing, cleaning and waste capacity</h2><p>The Purple Guide says provision should be made for cleaning and emptying toilets during prolonged events. A festival plan should identify who monitors each toilet area and when servicing can occur safely.</p><ul><li>Agree cleaning and emptying intervals</li><li>Plan spare consumables and cleaning materials</li><li>Provide attendants or inspection rounds where appropriate</li><li>Identify waste capacity and tanker access</li><li>Keep a fault and incident reporting process</li><li>Provide backup units or a supplier contingency</li></ul></section>
<section id="handwashing"><h2>Handwashing facilities</h2><p>The Purple Guide public summary says hand wash or gel should be provided. Position appropriate facilities close to each toilet area and keep them stocked throughout the event.</p><p>Food handlers and workers may need separate welfare arrangements from the public provision.</p></section>
<section id="contingency"><h2>Contingency planning</h2><p>Festival sanitation can be affected by weather, vehicle restrictions, higher attendance, breakdowns and unexpectedly heavy usage.</p><ul><li>Plan for attendance above the central estimate where appropriate</li><li>Agree emergency contact arrangements with the supplier</li><li>Identify replacement or reserve units</li><li>Protect servicing access in wet weather</li><li>Monitor queues and usage during the event</li><li>Review the plan after each event day</li></ul></section>
<section id="checklist"><h2>Festival toilet planning checklist</h2><div class="checklist"><label><input type="checkbox" /> Maximum arena attendance confirmed</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Opening hours and alcohol/food provision confirmed</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Campsite numbers confirmed</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Accessible units and routes planned</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Toilet zones marked on the site plan</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Handwashing provision specified</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Cleaning and emptying timetable agreed</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Service vehicle access protected</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Lighting and signage planned</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Fault and emergency plan agreed</label></div></section>
<section class="sources-box"><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li><a href="https://www.thepurpleguide.co.uk/sanitation" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Purple Guide sanitation guidance</a></li><li><a href="https://www.thepurpleguide.co.uk/accessibility" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Purple Guide accessibility guidance</a></li><li><a href="https://www.dover.gov.uk/Community/EventPlanning/A-Z/SanitaryFacilities.aspx" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">Dover District Council event sanitation guidance</a></li></ul></section><section id="faqs"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq-list"><details><summary>How many toilets are needed for a festival?</summary><p>The answer depends on attendance, duration, food and alcohol, campsites and servicing. The Purple Guide public summary gives a typical starting point of one female toilet per 100 attendees, one male toilet per 500 and one urinal per 150.</p></details><details><summary>Do festivals need accessible toilets?</summary><p>Yes. The Purple Guide public sanitation summary says at least one dedicated accessible toilet should be provided, and accessibility guidance says facilities should be clearly signposted.</p></details><details><summary>How often should festival toilets be serviced?</summary><p>The appropriate frequency depends on use and event duration. Prolonged events need planned cleaning and emptying during the event.</p></details><details><summary>Do festival campsites need separate toilets?</summary><p>Campsites create different demand patterns and should have suitable provision close to camping areas, including accessible facilities.</p></details></div></section><section class="find-suppliers" id="find-suppliers"><h2>Find portable toilet hire companies</h2><p>Browse suppliers serving towns and cities across the UK.</p><a class="primary-button" href="/locations/">Browse toilet hire locations</a></section><section class="disclaimer"><h2>Important</h2><p>This guide provides general planning information. Check current official guidance and confirm the final specification with the venue, relevant authority and an experienced supplier.</p></section></article></div></main></Layout>
